  "A nil under MY light. Every kid on this block just felt that in their teeth. Brick felt it too — look at her, almost interested.": [
   677130,
   8685
  ],
  "Match point at the Pj’s. Understand: this is the hand the whole block stops cooking to watch. I can feel ’em at the fences.": [
   686065,
   8015
  ],
  "Last hand under this light. Whatever the cards do — the girl who plays it ain’t leaving here a tourist. Not anymore.": [
   694330,
   8300
  ],
  "Their hand. Under my light. Brick, if you laugh, I swear—": [
   702880,
   3505
  ],
  "The Pj’s take it. The streetlight keeps what it catches, kids. That’s been true since before your route had a map.": [
   706635,
   6735
  ],
  "Rematch, first hand, my light. You know what this light’s seen. Add something.": [
   713620,
   5070
  ],
  "Cards out under the light. First looks tell me everything on this street. I’ve had twenty years of practice reading ’em.": [
   718940,
   8340
  ],
  "Big bid under the light. The fences just got quieter. That’s how you know it registered.": [
   727530,
   4455
  ],
  "JOKER. Under my light. The whole street just looked up from whatever it was pretending to do.": [
   732235,
   7165
  ],
  "Cut my book on my own street. …Brick’s eyebrows moved. That’s her version of a standing ovation, so take it and keep moving.": [
   739650,
   7200
  ],
  "That busted nil from earlier is still in this light. The street saw it. The street’s still deciding what it means for your name.": [
   747100,
   6520
  ],
  "After a swing like that, this stops being a stop on a route. It’s a story with a fence full of witnesses.": [
   753870,
   6190
  ],
  "Back under my light. The street kept your seats and the score kept its meaning. Finish what’s open.": [
   760310,
   6390
  ],
  "Queenie felt that in ’87 too. She just smiled the same.": [
   766950,
   3450
  ],
  "That’s how runs END, right there. I’ve stood where you’re standing.": [
   770650,
   4350
  ],
  "Redd came up the block. June’s by the fence. Word travels, baby — tonight’s table is the whole neighborhood’s business.": [
   775250,
   7995
  ],
  "They hear you, June. Eyes up, everybody — lesson’s running.": [
   783495,
   3920
  ],
  "I stood where you’re standing, young blood. That door don’t open for everybody who knocks.": [
   787665,
   5690
  ],
  "Walking, then. Walk well — we’ll be watching the whole way.": [
   793605,
   3425
  ],
  "Frederick Douglass. Taught himself to read when it was forbidden to him, escaped slavery at twenty, and wrote it all down for the world to answer for.": [
   797280,
   6980
  ],
  "Harriet Tubman. She got herself free — then went back some thirteen times for family and strangers alike, and she brought out every single one.": [
   804510,
   6820
  ],
  "Nat Turner. In 1831 he rose in Southampton County and led the deadliest slave rebellion in American history.": [
   811580,
   6775
  ],
  "Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Twenty-six years old when Montgomery stopped riding the buses — and he led that boycott all 381 days.": [
   818605,
   7795
  ],
  "Sojourner Truth. Born enslaved — and the first Black woman to win a court case against a white man in this country, bringing her own son home.": [
   826650,
   7795
  ],
  "Fannie Lou Hamer. A Mississippi sharecropper who stood before a national convention in 1964 and told the truth until the whole country had to listen.": [
   834695,
   8340
  ],
  "Marcus Garvey. He built the largest Black movement of his age — and put our own ships on the water with the Black Star Line.": [
   843285,
   5450
  ],
  "Ida B. Wells. A mob wrecked her newspaper office for printing the truth about lynching — so she carried that truth north and printed it louder.": [
   848985,
   7430
  ],
  "W.E.B. Du Bois. The first Black man to earn a Ph.D. from Harvard, in 1895 — and he spent ninety-five years writing the record straight.": [
   856665,
   7640
  ],
  "Malcolm X. He remade himself in a prison cell — walked out teaching discipline, dignity, and standing up straight.": [
   864555,
   6770
  ],
  "Angela Davis. A philosopher they put on trial in 1972 — she walked out acquitted, and she has been teaching ever since.": [
   871575,
   6915
  ],
  "Huey P. Newton. In 1966, in Oakland, he co-founded the Black Panther Party — and a neighborhood's protection became a movement.": [
   878740,
   8385
  ],
  "Hold on now. That's Malcolm they just cut. The man told us freedom would come 'by any means necessary' — a spade on a playing card changes none of it.": [
   887375,
   7365
  ],
  "Malcolm X, cut down on a trick. He taught a whole generation to stand up straight — 'by any means necessary' was never a slogan. It was a promise.": [
   894990,
   7745
  ],
